<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · British Isles</p><h1 class="st-title">Iceni</h1><p class="st-meta">50 BCE – 61 CE (approx.) · capital Venta Icenorum (Caistor St Edmund)</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">East Anglian kingdom whose queen Boudica led the great revolt of 60 or 61, burning Camulodunum, Londinium and Verulamium before Rome crushed the rising.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The Iceni held <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norfolk">Norfolk</a> and parts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suffolk">Suffolk</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambridgeshire">Cambridgeshire</a>; they are probably the Cenimagni who surrendered to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/julius-caesar">Julius Caesar</a> in 54 BCE. In the 1st century BCE their elite deposited spectacular gold torcs at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/snettisham-hoard">Snettisham</a>, and from about 10 BCE they issued <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> coins, some inscribed ECEN, with horse designs.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Coins name rulers such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antedios">Antedios</a>, and settlements like <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thetford">Thetford</a> and Saham Toney appear to have been major centres. After the Roman conquest of 43 the Iceni became a client kingdom, but they rebelled in 47 when the governor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/publius-ostorius-scapula">Ostorius Scapula</a> tried to disarm them. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prasutagus">Prasutagus</a> later ruled as a client king, enjoying Roman favour while keeping horse-rearing and an independent elite.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prasutagus">Prasutagus</a> died around 60, leaving half his realm to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nero">Nero</a>, Roman officials seized it, flogged his widow <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boudica">Boudica</a> and raped her daughters. Boudica led the Iceni and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/trinovantes">Trinovantes</a> in revolt and destroyed three cities before <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaius-suetonius-paulinus">Suetonius Paulinus</a> defeated her. The territory was annexed and later organised as a civitas centred on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venta-icenorum">Venta Icenorum</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/iceni.jpg" alt="Iceni" width="320" height="453"><figcaption>Image: Original uploader was Yorkshirian at · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Map_of_the_Territory_of_the_Iceni.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>50 BCE – 61 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/1st-century-bce">1st century BCE</a> – <a href="/centuries/1st-century">1st century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Venta Icenorum (Caistor St Edmund)</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Antedios, Prasutagus, Boudica</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Celtic polytheism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Common Brittonic</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/united-kingdom">United Kingdom</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Eceni, Kingdom of Prasutagus, Cenimagni</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=6&amp;focus=iceni" aria-label="Iceni on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4702 367 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Iceni lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5022 623L5304 942M5022 623L5020 640"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5304 942h0M5020 640h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5022 623h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5022 623h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=6&amp;focus=iceni">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
